# Migration Prompt: Express + routing-controllers → Fastify (two-stage, function-based)

> Paste this whole document to your coding agent as the task brief. It is written for an agent that has **read/write access to the repository** and can run the build and tests. Where it says "inspect," the agent must read the actual code rather than assume — the stack notes below are the starting map, not the territory.

> **What changed from the previous version of this brief:** the migration is now explicitly **two stages**. Stage 0 removes `routing-controllers` and `typedi` **while staying on Express** — you reach function-based, decorator-free parity behind the frozen wire contract *before* the HTTP layer changes. Stage 1 is the Express→Fastify swap (the plan you already had), now starting from clean function handlers. This decouples two independent hard changes so neither hides the other's regressions.

---

## 1. Mission

You are migrating an internal Node.js **wrapper/framework package** (a shared library other services depend on) from **Express 4 + `routing-controllers` 0.11.3 + `typedi`** to **Fastify 5, function-based**, with the explicit goal of **higher request throughput**. You will do it in two stages:

- **Stage 0 — De-decorate on Express (same framework).** Delete the decorator-based routing/param/DI model entirely and replace it with plain Express routers, function handlers, module-level services, and `AsyncLocalStorage` request context. **You stay on Express.** `class-validator`, `reflect-metadata`, and `express` all remain. The goal of this stage is a decorator-free, function-based package that passes the full contract suite unchanged.
- **Stage 1 — Swap Express → Fastify.** Replace the HTTP, validation-schema, serialization, and middleware layers with Fastify 5 as previously planned, now starting from function handlers rather than decorators.

This is **not** a driver swap. `routing-controllers` does not support Fastify, so its decorator/metadata layer is being deleted, not re-pointed — and in this plan it is deleted in Stage 0, before Fastify is introduced. Treat both stages as rewrites of the HTTP/validation/middleware layers while **preserving the package's external behavior and the wire contract of every endpoint**.

**Why up front:** `routing-controllers` couples four concerns that are usually thought of separately — routing, param binding, DI resolution (`typedi`), and *auto-triggering `class-validator`* on `@Body()` DTOs. Removing it breaks all four at once. Doing that on Express first means you reproduce those four behaviors (function routing, direct param access, plain-module/ALS DI, and **manual** validation invocation) against a framework you're not simultaneously replacing, so any contract regression is unambiguously attributable to the de-decorating, not to Fastify.

