## 6. Detailed migration nuances

### 6.1 Decorators → function handlers (the core Stage-0 change)

Delete `routing-controllers` controllers. **In Stage 0** each controller class becomes a **plain Express router module**; **in Stage 1** that same module becomes a **Fastify plugin**. Map the param decorators to direct request access — identically in both frameworks:

| routing-controllers | Stage 0 (Express) | Stage 1 (Fastify) |
|---|---|---|
| `@JsonController('/users')` + method decorators | `const r = Router(); r.get(...)` mounted at `/users` | a plugin that calls `fastify.get/post/...('/users/...', opts, handler)` |
| `@Get('/:id')` / `@Post()` / … | `r.get('/:id', ...)` etc. | `fastify.get('/:id', ...)` etc. |
| `@ReqPathParam('id')` / `@Param('id')` | `req.params.id` | `request.params.id` |
| `@ReqQueryParam('q')` / `@QueryParam('q')` | `req.query.q` | `request.query.q` |
| `@ReqHeaderParam('h')` / `@HeaderParam('h')` | `req.headers['h']` | `request.headers['h']` |
| `@Body()` | `req.body` (validate manually, §0.5) | `request.body` (validated by schema) |
| `@Req()` / `@Res()` | `req` / `res` | `request` / `reply` |
| `@CurrentUser()` | `req.user`, set by auth middleware (§0.7) | `request.user`, set by an auth `preHandler` (§6.4) |
| `@HttpCode(201)` | `res.status(201)` | `reply.code(201)` or `response` schema keyed by status |
| return value (auto-serialized) | `res.json(value)` | `return value` (Fastify serializes via the response schema) |
| throwing `NotFoundError`, … | throw → plain Express error middleware (§6.8) | throw `httpErrors.notFound()` / set `reply.code()` + throw (§6.8) |

**Shapes at each stage:**
```ts
// BEFORE — routing-controllers
@JsonController('/users')
export class UserController {
  constructor(private svc: UserService) {}        // typedi injection
  @Get('/:id') @HttpCode(200)
  async getUser(@ReqPathParam('id') id: string,
                @ReqQueryParam('full') full: boolean) {
    const u = await this.svc.find(id, full);
    if (!u) throw new NotFoundError('user not found');
    return u;
  }
}

// STAGE 0 — plain Express router, function handler, manual validation, no decorators/DI
import { Router } from 'express';
const svc = makeUserService(/* deps */);            // module singleton, see §6.4
export const userRouter = Router();
userRouter.get('/:id', async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const id = req.params.id;
    const full = req.query.full === 'true';         // reproduce RC's coercion
    const u = await svc.find(id, full);
    if (!u) throw new NotFoundError('user not found'); // caught by error middleware (§6.8)
    res.status(200).json(u);
  } catch (e) { next(e); }
});
// mounted elsewhere: app.use('/users', userRouter);

// STAGE 1 — plugin registered on the package's `nodecore` instance (see §6.1a),
//            schema-driven, compiled serializer
import type { NodeCorePluginAsync } from '@your-org/nodecore'; // the package's own type
import { Type } from '@sinclair/typebox';

export const userRoutes: NodeCorePluginAsync = async (nodecore) => {
  const svc = makeUserService(/* deps */);          // plain module factory, no typedi

  nodecore.get('/users/:id', {
    schema: {
      params: Type.Object({ id: Type.String() }),
      querystring: Type.Object({ full: Type.Optional(Type.Boolean()) }), // coercion via schema
      response: { 200: UserSchema, 404: ErrorSchema }, // 200 schema => compiled serializer
    },
  }, async (request, reply) => {
    const { id } = request.params;
    const { full } = request.query;
    const u = await svc.find(id, full ?? false);
    if (!u) throw nodecore.httpErrors.notFound('user not found'); // setErrorHandler shapes body
    return u;                                          // serialized via response schema
  });

  nodecore.post('/users', {
    schema: {
      body: CreateUserSchema,                          // replaces class-validator on the DTO
      response: { 201: UserSchema },
    },
  }, async (request, reply) => {
    reply.code(201);                                   // replaces @HttpCode(201)
    return svc.create(request.body);
  });
};
```
Note: in Stage 1, validation/coercion moves **before** the handler via the schema. Remove the Stage-0 manual coercion/validation from the handler body, but **only** once the schema reproduces the same constraints. All route registration and instance-level calls (`.get/.post/...`, `.httpErrors`, hooks, decorators, `.register`) go through the `nodecore` instance parameter, **never** a bare `fastify` — see §6.1a.

### 6.1a The `nodecore` instance convention (public boundary)

Every route plugin receives the package's HTTP instance as a parameter named **`nodecore`**, not `fastify`. This is the single public boundary between consumers and the underlying framework:

- **Plugins are typed as `NodeCorePluginAsync`** (the package's exported alias), and the instance is `NodeCoreInstance`. Consumers import these from the package (`@your-org/nodecore`), never from `fastify`. Do not re-export raw `FastifyInstance`/`FastifyPluginAsync` from the entry point.
- **All instance-level calls go through `nodecore`:** `nodecore.get/post/put/delete/patch`, `nodecore.register`, `nodecore.addHook`, `nodecore.decorate`, `nodecore.setErrorHandler`, `nodecore.httpErrors.*`. There should be **no bare `fastify.` reference inside a plugin.** (This is why the example's error throw is `nodecore.httpErrors.notFound(...)`, not `fastify.httpErrors...`.)
- **Decide the abstraction depth up front (this is a §8.3 call):**
  - *Type-alias (minimal):* `export type NodeCoreInstance = FastifyInstance` and `export type NodeCorePluginAsync = FastifyPluginAsync`. Cheapest, and it already stops consumers importing `fastify` by name — but the *shape* is still Fastify's, so a future Fastify major could leak through.
  - *Curated facade (stronger):* export a `NodeCoreInstance` interface exposing only the methods you support, backed by a Fastify instance internally. More work, but Fastify becomes a true implementation detail and `request`/`reply` handler types are yours to control.
  - Pick one, document it in §8, and apply it uniformly. Whichever you choose, the **handler signature `(request, reply)` types are part of the public contract** — pin them to package-owned types if you want Stage-1-and-beyond framework swaps to stay non-breaking.
- The package's bootstrap constructs the real `fastify({ ... })` once, wraps/brands it as `NodeCoreInstance`, and hands *that* to `@fastify/autoload`. Plugins never see or construct the raw Fastify object.
- **Stage 0 alignment:** if consumers already register routes through a package helper in the Express phase, name and shape that helper so its Stage-0 signature matches the Stage-1 `nodecore` surface as closely as possible — otherwise the handler-arg types change between stages and you incur the second public break §8.3 warns about.

### 6.2 Validation

**Stage 0:** `class-validator` stays. The only change is that *you* now trigger it (RC used to). Reproduce RC's configured `validation` options exactly and throw the same error shape (§6.8). Do not convert to JSON Schema yet.

**Stage 1:** each `class-validator` DTO becomes a schema. Preserve the **exact** constraints — do not loosen them. Reference mapping:

| class-validator | schema |
|---|---|
| `@IsString()` | `type: 'string'` |
| `@IsInt()` / `@IsNumber()` | `type: 'integer'` / `type: 'number'` |
| `@IsBoolean()` | `type: 'boolean'` (with coercion for querystring) |
| `@IsEmail()` | `format: 'email'` |
| `@Min(n)` / `@Max(n)` | `minimum` / `maximum` |
| `@Length(a,b)` / `@MinLength` / `@MaxLength` | `minLength` / `maxLength` |
| `@IsEnum(E)` | `enum: [...]` |
| `@IsOptional()` | omit from `required` |
| `@ValidateNested()` + `@Type(() => X)` | nested object schema |
| `@IsArray()` + element decorators | `type: 'array', items: {...}` |
| custom validators (`FutureDate`, `PastDate`, `BetweenDates`, `ValidDate`) | **custom AJV keywords/formats** or TypeBox refinements |

**Custom validators** (`FutureDate`, etc.): in Stage 0 they keep working as-is (they're `class-validator` decorators, independent of RC). In Stage 1, register them as AJV custom keywords/formats on the Fastify AJV instance, or as a `preValidation` check that throws the same error. Do **not** drop the semantics. If a custom validator encoded business rules (e.g. "date must be after today"), reproduce it precisely and unit-test it against the old behavior.

Set AJV options (Stage 1) to match prior behavior where it matters (`coerceTypes`, `removeAdditional`, `useDefaults`). Decide explicitly whether unknown properties were previously allowed (RC's `whitelist`/`forbidNonWhitelisted`); mirror that with `additionalProperties`.

### 6.3 Middleware → the right stage in each framework

**Stage 0:** convert RC `@Middleware`/`ExpressMiddlewareInterface` classes to plain Express `(req,res,next)` middleware, preserving registration order and per-route scoping.

**Stage 1:** map each Express middleware to the correct Fastify lifecycle stage — `onRequest → preParsing → preValidation → preHandler → handler → preSerialization → onSend → onResponse` — **not** blindly to `onRequest`:

| Middleware | Fastify hook | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `ContextMiddleware` (request context / correlation) | `onRequest` + `AsyncLocalStorage` | establish the request store early; §6.4 |
| auth / token validation | `preHandler` (or a route-scoped decorator) | sets `request.user` |
| request/response logging middleware | mostly **removed** → Pino `onResponse` | keep only custom fields |
| `TimeoutMiddleware` | route `connectionTimeout` / `preHandler` w/ `AbortController` | per-route timeouts are cleaner here |
| `NoCacheMiddleware` | `onSend` (set `Cache-Control`) or `@fastify/helmet` | header mutation belongs in `onSend` |
| body/raw capture | `preParsing` / content-type parser | §6.11 |

Scope hooks to the plugin/route where they applied, not globally. Verify the **effective order** matches the old chain for any route where ordering had observable effects (e.g. auth before rate-limit vs after).

### 6.4 DI removal (`typedi`) + request context — done in Stage 0

`routing-controllers` resolved controllers and `@CurrentUser`/services through `typedi`. Going function-based (Stage 0), choose per service lifetime:

- **Singletons** (`@Service()` with no request state) → plain module-level singletons or factory functions. Simplest; prefer this.
- **Request-scoped state** (correlation IDs, per-request user/tenant, anything `ContextMiddleware` stored on the request) → **`AsyncLocalStorage`**. Establish the store in an early Express middleware in Stage 0; in Stage 1 the *same* store is established in an `onRequest` hook. This replaces typedi's request container and is the idiomatic Node approach across both frameworks.
- You **may** keep `typedi` purely as a DI container decoupled from routing if the service graph is large — but if you do, document why, and ensure it doesn't drag `reflect-metadata` decorator usage back into the routing layer. (Note: `reflect-metadata` is present anyway until Stage 1 because of `class-validator`.)

Whichever you choose, **preserve service lifetimes** — turning a request-scoped service into a singleton (or vice-versa) is a behavior change and a potential cross-request data-leak bug. Flag any service whose scope is ambiguous.

### 6.6 GraphQL: Apollo Server 4 → `@as-integrations/fastify` (Stage 1)

- Install `@as-integrations/fastify`. Construct `ApolloServer` and **include `fastifyApolloDrainPlugin(fastify)` in its `plugins`** array (required for graceful shutdown / draining).
- `await apollo.start()` **before** registering the integration.
- Apollo 4 moved context out of the constructor: provide a context function `(request, reply) => Promise<Context>` (typed `FastifyRequest`/`FastifyReply`). Port whatever the old context builder did (auth, loaders, tenant) into this function. (In Stage 0, keep the existing Express Apollo middleware wiring untouched — GraphQL moves only in Stage 1.)
- Register either as a plugin on the existing GraphQL path, or via `fastifyApolloHandler` inside an explicit route. Keep the **same path and methods** as today.
- **Subscriptions:** Apollo Server 4 does **not** handle WS subscriptions itself. If the current setup has GraphQL subscriptions, that is a separate `graphql-ws` + `@fastify/websocket` wiring — **do not assume it carries over; flag it** (§9).

### 6.7 WebSockets and SSE

- **Stage 0:** replace `WebSocketDecorators` with direct `ws` server wiring; preserve auth and message handling.
- **Stage 1 — WebSockets:** move to `@fastify/websocket` (built on `ws`). Use `fastify.get('/path', { websocket: true }, (connection, request) => { ... })`. The connection/lifecycle API differs from raw `ws`; port handlers accordingly and preserve auth (do it in `preValidation`/`onRequest` for the upgrade route).
- **Stage 1 — SSE (`SSE.ts`) is not WebSockets.** It's a long-lived `text/event-stream` HTTP response. Implement as a route that writes to `reply.raw` (or use a small SSE plugin), sets the right headers, and handles client disconnect. Keep the event format and heartbeat behavior identical. Migrate SSE and WS as **separate** concerns.

### 6.8 Error handling + error-shape contract

- `routing-controllers` had `HttpError` subclasses and a global error handler producing a specific JSON error shape. **Capture that exact shape first** (status code + body) from the current code/tests, in Stage 0 §0.1.
- **Stage 0:** reproduce that shape in a plain Express error-handling middleware, and reproduce the validation-error shape from your manual `class-validator` invocation. This interim Express implementation becomes a concrete, tested reference for Stage 1.
- **Stage 1:** set a global `setErrorHandler` (and optionally `@fastify/sensible`'s `httpErrors`) that reproduces the same body for the same conditions. Override Fastify's default 400 validation body via `setErrorHandler` and/or `schemaErrorFormatter` to match the shape you preserved in Stage 0. This is a contract item, not cosmetic.
- Ensure thrown errors still get traced/reported to APM (§6.10) in both stages.

### 6.9 Logging: drop `morgan` → built-in Pino (Stage 1)

- `morgan` stays through Stage 0. In Stage 1, configure Fastify's Pino logger. Set `genReqId` to derive from the incoming correlation header if one exists, and wire `request.id` to the OTel trace ID so logs ↔ traces correlate.
- **Banking/PII redaction is mandatory.** Use Pino's `redact` to strip auth headers, tokens, account numbers, PANs, and any sensitive body fields. Inventory what the current logging exposed and ensure the new logs are at least as redacted (preferably more).
- Reproduce any log fields downstream systems parse (request id, latency, status, route). Remove `morgan` only after parity is confirmed.

### 6.10 Observability preservation (critical ordering)

- **Init order:** the OTel SDK (`@opentelemetry/sdk-node`) and instrumentations must be **started before the HTTP framework is imported/instantiated**, or auto-instrumentation won't attach. This is true in both stages — verify the tracing bootstrap still loads first (e.g. `node -r ./dist/tracing.js`) after Stage 0's router changes and again after Stage 1.
- Stage 1: add `@opentelemetry/instrumentation-fastify` (confirm it's enabled within `auto-instrumentations-node`) so Fastify routes produce spans equivalent to the old Express instrumentation.
- `elastic-apm-node` auto-instruments both Express and Fastify; confirm transactions are named by route and errors are captured in each stage.
- `prom-client`: keep the existing registry/metrics, or adopt `fastify-metrics` (Stage 1). Either way, **preserve metric names and labels** so dashboards/alerts keep working.

### 6.11 Raw body / webhook signature verification (Stage 1 gotcha)

If `raw-body` was used to verify webhook/HMAC signatures (common for payment/bank callbacks), Express currently gives you the raw bytes; Fastify parses JSON before your handler sees it, destroying them. In Stage 1 use **`@fastify/raw-body`** (or a custom `addContentTypeParser` with `parseAs: 'buffer'`) on the specific signature-verifying routes to retain the raw buffer **alongside** the parsed body. Losing the raw body silently breaks signature checks — verify these routes explicitly. (Stage 0 leaves `raw-body` intact.)

### 6.12 Routing discovery

- **Stage 0:** replace `routing-controllers`' controller discovery with explicit route-module registration (`app.use(prefix, router)`), preserving every base path/`routePrefix`.
- **Stage 1:** replace that with **`@fastify/autoload`** pointed at the routes/plugins directory. Preserve route prefixes via autoload's `dirNameRoutePrefix` or explicit prefixes so URLs don't change.

### 6.13 Dual CJS + ESM preservation (both stages)

- Keep the package's existing module system and build output at every step. Express, Fastify, and all `@fastify/*` plugins are dual, so this is achievable.
- In **CJS** use `require('@fastify/x')` / `fastify.register(require('@fastify/x'))`; the `await fastify.register(import('@fastify/x'))` form in some docs is ESM-only — don't mix it into a CJS build.
- Before merging **each stage**, build and import the package both ways (a `require()` smoke test and an `import` smoke test) to prove dual consumption still works. Do not add any ESM-only dependency without flagging it (§9).

